<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
    <dict>
        <key>Description</key>
        <string>This download is NOT verified yet: GitHub records no digest for the release's file, the release's text names no hash and no signature, and a check of the signature is not built, so nothing proves that the file is what the project published. The shared importer therefore refuses a real run of the cimian recipe unless the override sets cimian_allow_unverified to true (pcman_allow_unverified for the package tool's recipe). Downloads the current release of XCP-ng Center for Windows (the .msi asset, named XCP-ng.Center.msi, of the newest non-pre-release on GitHub that has a matching file, kept under the asset's own file name). The release list was read on 2026-10-04: the newest release, v25.04 (published 2025-04-29), has the MSI with no digest, and no release of the list has one. A digest step (GitHubAssetDigest, then ChecksumVerifier) can come back into this recipe when a release has a digest. The first run with AutoPkg 2.9.0, on 2026-10-04, took the installer of release v20.04.01.33 (named like XCP-ng-Center-version.msi), a file of December 2020, under the earlier pattern and stopped for a missing digest: AutoPkg takes the first release that has a matching file however old it is, so a new spelling of the name would send the recipe back to an older release; read the line that names the selected asset and release in the first dry run. The recipe never stops on an unchanged download, so a failed import is tried again at the next run. Pre-releases are skipped unless the override sets the input include_prereleases to true. The step GitHubNewestReleaseGuard, right after the provider, stops the run when the release that the provider selected is not the newest release (a pre-release is skipped unless include_prereleases is true): it names the newest release and its files. The override's input accepted_newest_release, set to the tag of a newest release that has no Windows installer file (.msi, .exe, .msix) on purpose, lets the run go on only while that release is the newest, and release_tag_pattern names the line of releases to follow for a vendor with two lines (every run names the newer releases that it left out).</string>
        <key>Identifier</key>
        <string>com.github.serrc-techops.download.XCPngCenter-Win</string>
        <key>Input</key>
        <dict>
            <key>NAME</key>
            <string>XCPngCenter</string>
            <key>SEARCH_PATTERN</key>
            <string>XCP-ng\.Center\.msi$</string>
            <key>accepted_newest_release</key>
            <string></string>
            <key>include_prereleases</key>
            <false/>
            <key>release_tag_pattern</key>
            <string></string>
        </dict>
        <key>MinimumVersion</key>
        <string>1.0.0</string>
        <key>Process</key>
        <array>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>GitHubReleasesInfoProvider</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>github_repo</key>
                    <string>xcp-ng/xenadmin</string>
                    <key>asset_regex</key>
                    <string>%SEARCH_PATTERN%</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>com.github.serrc-techops.SharedProcessors/GitHubNewestReleaseGuard</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>github_asset_api_url</key>
                    <string>%asset_url%</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>URLDownloader</string>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>EndOfCheckPhase</string>
            </dict>
        </array>
    </dict>
</plist>
